    "body": "\"Documents are allowed to use \"U+1F914 THINKING FACE (🤔)\" as well, though this is somewhat verbose.\" is hardly an explanation.\r\n\r\nOr did you mean, \"U+0029 can be referred to as \"U+0029 RIGHT PARENTHESIS\", because even though it renders, this avoids unmatched parentheses.\"?\r\n\r\nUnmatched parentheses are better avoided by editing and proofreading with care than by surrendering to careless efforts.\r\n\r\nRather than an argument in favor of leaving out the example characters, I think this is an argument in favor of more obvious styling, such as --\r\n\r\n- A multiple assignment syntax can be used to assign multiple variables to the <a>tuple</a>'s <a for=tuple>items</a>, by surrounding the variable names with `U+0028` `LEFT PARENTHESIS` (`(`) and `U+0029` `RIGHT PARENTHESIS` (`)`), and separating each variable name with a `U+002C` `COMMA` (`,`). The number of variables assigned cannot differ from the number of <a for=tuple>items</a> in the <a>tuple</a>.\r\n\r\n- For notational convenience, a literal syntax can be used to express <a>lists</a>, by surrounding the list with `U+00AB` `LEFT-POINTING DOUBLE ANGLE QUOTATION MARK ` (`«`) and `U+00BB` `RIGHT-POINTING DOUBLE ANGLE QUOTATION MARK` (`»`), and separating its <a for=list>items</a> with `U+002C` `COMMA` (`,`). An indexing syntax can be used by providing a zero-based index into a list inside square brackets (`[` and `]`). The index cannot be out-of-bounds, except when used with <a for=list>exists</a>.\r\n\r\nStyling gets better and more obvious when CSS similar to that used by W3C is in play.",
